{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T10:03:51.948337", "user_message": "Differentiate f(x) = x^3 - 4x + 7. Return the derivative and one short justification.", "final_answer": "The derivative of \\( f(x) = x^3 - 4x + 7 \\) is \\( f'(x) = 3x^2 - 4 \\). This can be justified by applying the power rule for differentiation, which states that if you have a function like \\( ax^n \\), its derivative is \\( anx^{n-1} \\). In this case, we differentiate each term separately: the derivative of \\( x^3 \\) is \\( 3x^2 \\), and the derivative of \\( -4x \\) is \\( -4 \\), leaving us with \\( f'(x) = 3x^2 - 4 \\).", "route": "general_conversation", "planner_used": "deterministic_academic_fast_path", "planner_validated": true, "llm_synthesis_used": true, "critic_passed": true, "memory_found": false, "response_time": 14.97}
